        "heading": "The DOL card path — and the reconciled OSHA wording",
        "html": "<p>Read this plainly, because two sources in the research disagreed and this course reconciles them: <strong>OSHA requires no test for the DOL card</strong>. The card comes from completing the required hours with an authorized trainer. Most authorized providers add quizzes and a final exam, and OSHA's program rules set those at <strong>70% with up to 3 attempts</strong>. The 70% on this course's OSHA practice exams is that <strong>provider-standard practice benchmark, NOT an OSHA pass mark</strong>. There is no federal OSHA exam, and the DOL card is not a license or a certification.</p><p>The hours: <strong>OSHA 10 is 10 contact hours over a minimum of 2 days</strong>; <strong>OSHA 30 is 30 contact hours over a minimum of 4 days</strong>. No more than <strong>7.5 hours per day</strong>, and the course must be completed within <strong>180 days</strong>. The trainer issues the DOL wallet card within <strong>90 days</strong>. There is no prerequisite — OSHA 30 can be taken without OSHA 10 — and the card has no federal expiry, though some states and cities set their own limits.</p>"
