        "html": "<p>A floor starts at the <strong>sill plate</strong>, bolted to the foundation. <strong>Floor joists</strong> sit on it — or on a <strong>girder</strong> at midspan. The <strong>rim joist</strong> closes the joist ends, ties the floor together, and carries the wall above. <strong>Blocking or bridging</strong> keeps joists from twisting and shares loads. The <strong>subfloor</strong> — usually 3/4-inch plywood or OSB at 16-inch joist spacing — is glued and nailed to the joists, which stops squeaks. A <strong>joist hanger</strong> is the metal bracket that connects a joist to a beam or ledger. Joist span is set by size, species, grade, spacing, and load.</p><p>Count joists the same way as studs: spaces plus one. A <strong>cantilever</strong> is a joist or beam that extends past its support. An <strong>I-joist</strong> is an engineered joist with flanges and a web, built for long, straight spans.</p>"
